@page "/learn/relative-navigation"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Relative links · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Relative links</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">An order page has tabs. Can each tab link to its neighbours without spelling out <code>/orders/12047/</code>?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Pages &amp; navigation · Any render mode · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> <code>NavLink</code> and <code>NavigateTo</code> gain a
        <code>RelativeToCurrentUri</code> option, which resolves a link against the current page instead of the
        app's root. It's off by default. .NET 10 had no such option: you spelled out the full path, or worked out
        the folder yourself.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is order 12047, at <code>…/orders/12047/summary</code>. It has three tabs: Summary, Lines and
        Customer. Each tab is a <code>NavLink</code> written the natural way, <code>href="lines"</code>, with no
        option set.
    </p>
    <p>
        This frame runs on .NET 11 with the option left off. That behaves exactly as .NET 10 did, which had no
        option to turn on; a real .NET 10 build lands in the same place. Watch the tabs, and the
        <b>You are at</b> line under them.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="Tabs written as plain relative links" Url="demo/relative-nav/net10/orders/12047/summary"
                Label='Option off, as in .NET 10' Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Before clicking, look at the tabs: none is underlined, though you're on Summary. Now click <b>Lines</b>.
            The address becomes <code>/lines</code>, and the app answers <b>Not Found</b>.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>Relative links start from the app's root</h2>
    <p>Every Blazor app has a base URL in its <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, and the browser resolves relative links against it:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Where href="lines" goes</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;base href="/" /&gt;                <span class="dim">in App.razor</span>
current page   /demo/relative-nav/net10/orders/12047/summary
href="lines"   →  /lines           <span class="err">the app's root, not the order</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        So <code>lines</code> means <code>/lines</code>, from any page. That's also why no tab is underlined:
        <code>NavLink</code> marks the tab whose URL matches the current one, and <code>/summary</code> doesn't.
        <code>NavigateTo</code> works the same way: in .NET 10, <code>NavigateTo("summary")</code> from an order
        redirects to <code>/summary</code>, a 404.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Resolve the link against the current page</h2>
    <p>Set <code>RelativeToCurrentUri</code> on each link, and pass the same option to <code>NavigateTo</code>.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;NavLink href="lines" <span class="hl">RelativeToCurrentUri="true"</span>&gt;Lines&lt;/NavLink&gt;

<span class="dim">// from …/orders/12047/lines, go to …/orders/12047/summary</span>
Navigation.NavigateTo("summary",
    <span class="hl">new NavigationOptions { RelativeToCurrentUri = true }</span>);</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        With the option on, everything after the current URL's last <code>/</code> is dropped, and your path goes
        on the end. From <code>…/12047/summary</code>, <code>lines</code> becomes <code>…/12047/lines</code>. The
        tabs need no order id and no path. Here's the same order page with the option on. It was opened at
        <code>…/orders/12047</code>, with no tab, and redirected itself to the Summary tab.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same tabs, relative to the page" Url="demo/relative-nav/orders/12047"
                Label=".NET 11: RelativeToCurrentUri" Side="after">
        <Caption>
            <b>Summary</b> is underlined, and <b>You are at</b> reads <code>…/orders/12047/summary</code>: the page
            redirected there with <code>NavigateTo</code>. Click <b>Lines</b>: the order's lines show, and
            <b>You are at</b> reads <code>/demo/relative-nav/orders/12047/lines</code>. Then click <b>← Orders</b>
            and open another order: the same tabs now point under it.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>
        Fig. 2's page also has an <b>← Orders</b> link back to a list, and Fig. 1 lives under <code>…/net10/…</code>
        so both can run in one app. Otherwise the two pages are the same, and only their tabs differ. Here are
        the tabs:
    </p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/RelativeLinks/OrderTabs.razor" Feature="relative-navigation">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's tabs: three relative links, and no parameters. Fig. 1's tabs are the same three
            <code>NavLink</code>s without <code>RelativeToCurrentUri</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <p>
        The redirect is where the option can catch you out. It drops everything after the last <code>/</code>, and
        on a page opened with no tab, that's the order id:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">NavigateTo with the option on, from three addresses</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">current page            path              goes to</span>
…/orders/12047/lines    "summary"      →  …/orders/12047/summary   <span class="ok">✓</span>
…/orders/12047/         "summary"      →  …/orders/12047/summary   <span class="ok">✓ trailing slash</span>
…/orders/12047          "summary"      →  …/orders/summary         <span class="err">the id was dropped</span>
…/orders/12047          "12047/summary" → …/orders/12047/summary   <span class="ok">✓ name the id again</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>Fig. 2's page is opened both ways, so it checks for the slash before it redirects:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/RelativeLinks/OrderPage.razor" Feature="relative-navigation">
        <Caption>
            The redirect in Fig. 2. With a trailing slash it navigates to <code>summary</code>; without one, to
            <code>12047/summary</code>, putting back the id the option drops. The tabs don't meet this: the
            page redirects before they're shown, so they always sit on a tab's own URL.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Spell out the path, or cut it up yourself</h2>
    <p>
        .NET 10 has no option to set. A .NET 10 build showed what three ways of writing an order's tabs do, on
        an order's Summary tab, <code>/orders/12047/summary</code>:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three ways to write a tab in .NET 10, and where it went</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;NavLink href="lines"&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">resolves against the app's root, and no tab is marked active</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ /lines: 404 Not found</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;NavLink href="orders/{Id}/lines"&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">works, but the tabs need the id and the whole path</span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ /orders/12047/lines</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;NavLink href="lines"
  <span class="err">RelativeToCurrentUri="true"</span>&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">compiles: NavLink passes attributes it doesn't know through to the &lt;a&gt;</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ still /lines, with no warning</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        <code>NavigationOptions.RelativeToCurrentUri</code> doesn't compile there at all: CS0117. The other
        workaround was string surgery, taking <code>Navigation.Uri</code>, cutting it at the last <code>/</code>,
        and adding the sibling's name, copied into every component that links to a sibling.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>One option, on NavLink and NavigateTo</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11, <code>NavLink.RelativeToCurrentUri</code> and <code>NavigationOptions.RelativeToCurrentUri</code>
        are both <code>bool</code>, and both default to <code>false</code>, so existing links behave as before.
        <code>NavLink</code> resolves the link while rendering, so it works on static pages too. It renders the
        full URL as its <code>href</code>, and matches that for its active tab. That's what Fig. 2 runs.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Mind the last segment.</b> It's dropped, even when it's an id rather than a page name, as the table above Fig. 4 shows. If a page can be reached with or without a trailing slash, check which before you navigate.</li>
        <li><b>Relative paths only.</b> With the option on, a path starting with <code>/</code>, or an absolute URL, throws <code>ArgumentException</code>.</li>
        <li><b><code>?</code> and <code>#</code> keep the page.</b> <code>?tab=2</code> replaces just the query, and <code>#notes</code> just the fragment.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
